  "notes": "Glossy hyper-bright dance-pop with a bubblegum topline. The drums are the entry's: \"hard-clipped four-on-the-floor kick, layered claps on 2&4, 16th hats, and white-noise sweeps as fills\", so the kick anchors all four beats at near-zero syncopation, the clap stack is doubled with a tight offset, `hihat.base` is 16th at a high fill density, and the fill lane is `riser` with the snare-roll ladder off. \"The Unholy mode substitutes a stomping half-time-feeling chant beat\" is documented but not encoded — a single `placement` field cannot hold both, and the four-on-the-floor is the lane the entry names first. The bass is a synth, not an 808: \"distorted saw sub pumping on 8ths; sub swell before drops\", so no `bass808` block is authored and the bassline is `offbeat_8ths` at a very high `followRootsProb`. Harmony is \"minor keys with a Eurodance i—VI—III—VII colour and a bright major pre-chorus lift\", carried as the top family plus a major one. The melody block encodes \"short, high, repetitive cells with a chant hook and heavy stacked doubles\" as the highest melody register in this batch, a small `motifPitchCount`, a 1-bar phrase and a high-density `octave_echo` counter for the doubles; \"countermelody is a pitched synth answer\" is the `answer_lick` weight. No `outro` length is declared, because the entry's ending is an \"abrupt full-stop ending\" and neither structure closes on one. ⚠ `confidence` is medium and the research says the parameters are \"lane-derived\": every drum probability, the humanize pair, the harmonic weights and `bpm.mode` 120 (the midpoint of the stated 110—130 band) are INFERRED. ⚠ The entry's [LOW-CONF] mark on the soprano/tuning claim is repeated here.",
